= Discussion

== What the Current Evidence Establishes

The semantic audit shows that one latent-map answer can remain possible without
being determined by the released text under the declared interpretation.
The certificate pipeline makes parts of the proposed supervision and evaluation
contract executable: evidence can be checked, menu decisions tied to semantic
domains, and unsupported construction rejected. These are implementation
properties, not demonstrated learning benefits.

== Limitations

The ontology excludes metric distance, navigation, visual grounding, and
unrestricted English. Solver and checker implementations share data definitions
and are not formally verified. Proof construction is incomplete even where the
semantic decision procedure applies. Acceptance filters and finite templates
select the generated distribution. Count evidence can remain exponential in
contingent memberships, so context admission can disproportionately remove hard
examples.

Natural and Symbolic expose intended certificate dependencies, but rendered
information and length require a corpus audit. Only Symbolic model outputs have
a replay parser. Process-valid outputs need not describe the model's
internal computation. Readability has not been measured with human readers.
Source signatures control a declared equivalence relation but cannot substitute
for predeclared structural holdouts. These limits constrain the conclusions of
any eventual pilot result.

== Next Empirical Steps

Freeze and inspect the admitted pilot data, run prompt-matched baselines, then
execute the supervised comparisons with development-only checkpoint selection.
Measure rejection and token distributions alongside accuracy. Audit the
rendered evidence mapping before attributing differences to notation alone.
Expand premise-first sampling and Count stress tests only with reported coverage
and extraction failures. Intervention studies and human explanation assessments
are separate follow-ups to external certificate validity.
